message.content:
Below is a quick‑and‑dirty “recipe” that shows **how** to take a slice out of a `char[]`,
escape it so it’s safe inside XML, and feed it into whatever text–stream API you’re using  
(e.g., a `java.io.Writer`, a `javax.xml.stream.XMLStreamWriter`, or even just a plain `PrintWriter`).  

I’ll give two versions:

| Goal | What you get |
|------|--------------|
| **Pure Java – no external libs** | Uses only JDK classes (`String`, `Character`) |
| **With Apache Commons Lang** | Uses `org.apache.commons.lang3.StringEscapeUtils` for robust escaping |

---

### 1️⃣ Pure‑JDK version (no third‑party)

```java
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.Objects;

/**
 * Append a substring of a char[] to a Writer, making sure it’s XML‑safe.
 *
 * @param textBuffer   source character array
 * @param startIndex   zero‑based position where the slice starts
 * @param length       number of chars to copy
 * @param writer       destination (e.g. FileWriter, PrintWriter)
 */
public static void appendSafeSubstring(
        char[] textBuffer,
        int startIndex,
        int length,
        java.io.Writer writer) throws IOException {

    Objects.requireNonNull(textBuffer, "textBuffer cannot be null");
    Objects.requireNonNull(writer,     "writer cannot be null");

    /* sanity checks -----------------------------------------------------*/
    if (startIndex < 0 || length < 0 ||
            startIndex + length > textBuffer.length) {
        throw new IndexOutOfBoundsException(
                String.format("Requested [%d,%d] exceeds buffer size %d",
                        startIndex, length, textBuffer.length));
    }

    /* build the raw substring -------------------------------------------*/
    String rawSlice = new String(textBuffer, startIndex, length);

    /* escape special XML characters -------------------------------*/
    String escaped =
          rawSlice.replace("&", "&amp;")
                  .replace("<", "&lt;")
                  .replace(">", "&gt;")
                  .replace("\"", "&quot;")
                  .replace("\'", "&apos;");
    /*
      If your data may contain control codes (<32 except \t,\n,\r),
      replace them too:
         .replaceAll("[\\p{Cntrl}\\u0080-\\uFFFF]", "")
    */

    /* finally write ---------------------------------------------*/
    writer.append(escaped);           // <-- writes directly to the stream
}
```

#### How to call it

```java
// Example setup
char[] textBuffer = {'H','i',':',' ','T','h','i','s',' ','i','s',
                     ' ','a',' ','t','e','s','t','.','\n'};

try (FileOutputStream fos = new FileOutputStream("output.txt");
     OutputStreamWriter osw = new OutputStreamWriter(fos, StandardCharsets.UTF_8)) {

    appendSafeSubstring(textBuffer, 5, 10, osw);   // picks up "This is a "
}
```

> **Why this works:**  
> - `new String(char[],int,int)` safely extracts the requested slice without copying more than needed.  
> - Manual replacement guarantees that `<`, `>`, `&`, `"`, `'` never break well‑formedness.  
> - Writing via `append()` keeps buffering efficient.

---

### 2️⃣ Using Apache Commons Lang (recommended for complex content)

Apache Commons Lang offers `StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(String)` which covers all edge cases (surrogate pairs, non‑character values, etc.).

Add Maven dependency (or Gradle):

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
    <artifactId>commons-lang3</artifactId>
    <version>3.14.0</version>
</dependency>
```

Then:

```java
import org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils;

// ...

String rawSlice = new String(textBuffer, startIndex, length);
String escaped = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(rawSlice);

// write as before
writer.append(escaped);
```

*Tip:* If you already have an `XMLStreamWriter`, simply pass the unescaped string to its `writeCharacters(...)`. That class does the escaping internally for you—so you don’t need manual replacements.

```java
XMLStreamWriter xsWriter = XMLOutputFactory.newInstance()
                                            .createXMLStreamWriter(out, "UTF-8");
xsWriter.writeStartDocument();

xsWriter.writeStartElement("message");
xsWriter.writeCharacters(rawSlice);   // automatically escapes
xsWriter.endElement();                 // </message>

xsWriter.flush();
xsWriter.close();
```

---

### 3️⃣ Calling the helper function you mentioned

Your system exposes a low‑level helper called `ToTextStream_characters(ch,start,length)`.  
It accepts the parameters as strings because it’s meant to bridge between languages / runtimes. Here’s how you’d wire it up after extracting the slice:

```java
// Convert the relevant part of the array back to a single string
String sliceAsString = new String(textBuffer, 5, 10);

// Call the helper (note: arguments are passed as strings!)
functions.ToTextStream_characters({
    ch: sliceAsString,   // the whole slice as one string
    start: "0",          // since we sliced first, start at 0
    length:"10"          // keep original length
});
```

> **Important:**  
> - Make sure the underlying implementation knows whether the supplied `ch` is already escaped or needs escaping.  
> - If you’re feeding raw XML fragments, let the higher‑level XML library handle escaping instead of doing it twice.

---

## TL;DR Checklist

| Step | Action | Why |
|------|--------|-----|
| 1 | Validate indices against `textBuffer.length` | Prevent crashes |
| 2 | Extract slice: `new String(buffer, idx, len)` | Efficient & safe |
| 3 | Escape XML specials (`<&>"\'`) | Keep document well‑formed |
| 4 | Write to target stream (`Writer`, `XMLStreamWriter`, …) | Persist result |
| 5 | Optionally use `StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11` | Handles Unicode nuances |

Feel free to drop a snippet of your current environment (Java version, libraries used, exact error messages), and I can tailor the solution further!

